            <ArticleTitle>Intracranial Erdheim-Chester Disease mimicking parafalcine meningioma: report of two cases and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1878-8750(17)31997-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.wneu.2017.11.074</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Erdheim-Chester disease (ECD) is a rare, non-Langerhans cell histiocytosis that typically occurs in middle-aged patients. It is usually characterized by multifocal osteosclerotic lesions of the long-bones, however many cases have extraskeletal involvement. Central nervous system (CNS) involvement is common, but isolated CNS involvement at presentation has rarely been reported. Here we report two cases of dural-based ECD mimicking meningioma on imaging with no other identified sites of disease.</AbstractText>
